[Student "S"]
{A square hand, no 4-card Major, and 9 points. This is the very hand that the book said to bid 2NT with. And when you do, partner says 3NT which you pass.

The contract would be 3NT played by West.

East plays 3NT. South leads the \C2. You must be living right, South gives you a trick with the opening lead.

Winner List: \S = 1 : \H = 2 : \D = 1 : \C = 4 :: Total = 8

You got a good break on the opening lead, now you need only one more winner. There is <b>NO CHANCE</b> of creating another winner in \Ss, \Hs or \Cs, so your second good break is that you know it will have to be \Ds. 

You <b>COULD</b> lead toward dummy's \DQ, finessing South for the \DK. That would be a perfectly good 50% chance. But there is an even better play if you look carefully. 

Play a \D from your hand and if South plays low insert the \D9. Much of the time North will win with the \DJ or \D10. If so you are no worse off, you play South for the \DK. But if it happens that South was dealt both the \DJ and \D10 then North will have to win that trick with the \DK, or perhaps not even win it at all!

As you see, the Deep Finesse of the \D9 pays off here, where the straightforward finesse of the \DQ would lose the contract. Two chances are definitely better than one.

But what if South has played the \D10, splitting his honors?

You would put on dummy's \DQ and North would win the \DK. But you aren't down yet! Later you lead another \D toward dummy's \D A 9 and finesse South for the \DJ. }
[Play "S"]
C2

